Types of Proxy / AOP in Spring

Interview topic usually covers two layers:

1) How Spring AOP applies aspects → Proxy-based (default) vs AspectJ weaving
2) Inside Spring proxies → JDK Dynamic Proxy vs CGLIB

────────────────────────────────────────
A) Proxy-based AOP (Spring AOP) — default in Spring Boot

Spring wraps your bean in a **proxy**. Cross-cutting logic (@Transactional, @Cacheable, custom @Aspect) runs on the proxy.

Client
  ↓
Proxy (advice: before/after/around)
  ↓
Target bean method

Example:
userService.createUser()
→ actually: Proxy → Aspect/Interceptor → real UserService.createUser()

Important limitations
- Typically **public** methods on Spring beans
- Only calls that go **through the proxy** are advised
- Self-invocation bypasses proxy:

public void methodA() {
    methodB(); // this.methodB() → AOP / @Transactional may NOT apply
}

Used for: transactions, caching, security, logging, retries — most Boot apps.

Note: `@Aspect` / AspectJ-style annotations are common, but runtime is still Spring’s **proxy** AOP unless you enable full AspectJ weaving.

────────────────────────────────────────
B) Types of Spring proxies (JDK vs CGLIB)

| Proxy type        | When used                                      | How it works                          |
| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| JDK Dynamic Proxy | Target implements an interface (classic case)  | Proxy implements the same interface   |
| CGLIB             | No interface, or class-based proxy preferred   | Proxy is a **subclass** of the target |

JDK idea:
Client → Proxy (implements UserService) → UserServiceImpl

CGLIB idea:
Client → UserService$$SpringCGLIB (extends UserService) → super.method()

Extra points:
- `final` class / `final` method → CGLIB cannot advise that method/class
- Spring Boot often defaults toward CGLIB class proxies for consistency
- Related detail file: `How does Spring create proxies internally`

────────────────────────────────────────
C) AspectJ AOP (bytecode weaving)

AspectJ weaves advice into the class bytecode (compile-time / load-time / etc.).
No Spring proxy required for those join points.

────────────────────────────────────────
How weaving works (the “how?”)

Spring AOP:
Your .class stays normal. Spring wraps a proxy at runtime.
methodB() called via this → no proxy → no advice.

AspectJ:
A weaver **rewrites the .class** so advice calls are inserted inside the method body
(or around join points). After weaving, the class itself contains the extra logic.

Before weave (conceptually):
void methodB() {
    // business code
}

After weave (conceptually):
void methodB() {
    LoggingAspect.logBefore();   // inserted by weaver
    // business code
    LoggingAspect.logAfter();
}

So even:
void methodA() {
    methodB();   // self call
}
still runs advice, because methodB’s bytecode already includes it — no proxy hop needed.

When weaving can happen:

| Mode              | When                                              | Idea |
| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------- | ---- |
| Compile-time (CTW)| During build (`ajc` / AspectJ Maven/Gradle plugin)| .java/.class → woven .class in the jar |
| Load-time (LTW)   | When JVM loads the class (javaagent)              | Classloader weaves as classes load |
| Binary weaving    | Post-compile on existing jars                     | Weave already-compiled classes |

“No Spring proxy required” means:
for those woven join points, interception is inside the class bytecode,
not Client → Proxy → Target.

(You can still use Spring with AspectJ; Spring may configure LTW, but the mechanism is weaving, not JDK/CGLIB proxy.)

────────────────────────────────────────
What do YOU need to do for weaving? (practical)

Important:
@Aspect + @EnableAspectJAutoProxy  → still Spring PROXY AOP (default Boot).
That is NOT full bytecode weaving.

To actually weave, pick one:

── Option 1: Load-Time Weaving (LTW) with Spring (common interview “how”)

1. Add dependencies (aspectjweaver, spring-instrument / aspects as needed)
2. Start JVM with AspectJ agent, e.g.:
   -javaagent:/path/to/aspectjweaver.jar
   (and often spring-instrument for Spring LTW)
3. Enable Spring LTW, e.g.:
   @EnableLoadTimeWeaving
   or spring.instruments / LoadTimeWeaver config
4. Write @Aspect (or .aj) and pointcuts
5. Run app → classes get woven as they load

── Option 2: Compile-Time Weaving (CTW)

1. Add AspectJ compiler plugin to Maven/Gradle (`aspectj-maven-plugin` / `ajc`)
2. Put aspects in the project
3. Build → plugin rewrites .class files into the jar
4. Run normally (no javaagent required for those woven classes)

── Option 3: Don’t weave (what most apps do)

Keep Spring Boot defaults:
- @EnableAspectJAutoProxy (auto in Boot for many features)
- @Transactional / @Cacheable / @Aspect on public bean methods
- Call through Spring proxy (or split beans) for self-invocation

You only set up weaving when you truly need private/self-call/constructor join points.

Client
  ↓
Class with aspect already woven in

Can advise (depending on setup):
- private / protected methods
- constructors
- field get/set
- internal method calls (self-invocation)

More powerful, more setup/complexity → less common in typical Spring Boot microservices.

────────────────────────────────────────
D) Comparison

| Feature          | Spring Proxy AOP              | AspectJ weaving                 |
| ---------------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| Mechanism        | Proxy around bean             | Bytecode weaving                |
| Self-invocation  | ❌ Bypasses proxy              | ✅ Can be advised                |
| Private methods  | ❌ (normal Spring AOP)         | ✅                               |
| Constructors     | ❌                             | ✅                               |
| Setup            | Easy (default Boot)           | More complex                    |
| Typical use      | TX, cache, security, logging  | Deep / special AOP needs        |

Performance: AspectJ avoids proxy indirection; Spring proxy overhead is usually small.
Don’t oversell “AspectJ is always faster” in interviews — prefer capability differences.

────────────────────────────────────────
E) What companies usually use

Most Spring Boot services → **Spring Proxy AOP** (JDK or CGLIB under the hood).
AspectJ weaving → only when you truly need non-proxy join points.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔑 Interview Answer (Short)

Spring AOP is proxy-based: Spring wraps beans (JDK interface proxy or CGLIB subclass) and intercepts external calls — so self-invocation bypasses advice.
AspectJ weaves aspects into bytecode and can advise private methods, constructors, and internal calls.
In Spring Boot we almost always use proxy AOP; AspectJ only for advanced cases.
